{0 Point logs and wide events}

Observe exposes one logging facility. Point logs automatically emit one event;
wide events accumulate context and emit once.

{1 Point logs}

Tagged text is the smallest form:

{[
[%observe.info text ~tag:"auth" "user logged in"]
]}

Format arguments remain lazy until admission:

{[
[%observe.debug text ~tag:"query" "%s" (explain_query query)]
]}

Open structured literals are concise for local event shapes:

{[
[%observe.info
  untyped
    {
      action = "cart_validated";
      cart_id = "cart-42";
      items = 2;
      customer = { plan = "team"; returning = true };
      coupons = [ "WELCOME"; "TEAM" ];
    }]
]}

Literals describe themselves. A dynamic expression supplies its normal type
description because PPX expansion occurs before OCaml type checking:

{[
[%observe.info
  untyped { action = "cart_validated"; items = Observe.Type.int item_count }]
]}

For reusable declared shapes, derive a type description and record schema:

{[
type phase = Started | Authorized of string [@@deriving observe]

type checkout = { cart_id : string; phase : phase } [@@deriving observe]

[%observe.info
  typed ~using:checkout_schema
    { cart_id = "cart-42"; phase = Authorized "auth-7" }]
]}

The annotation-free typed record is checked by OCaml. Variants preserve their
constructor meaning in pretty output while JSON uses the documented structural
projection.

{1 Manual point authoring}

The PPX shortens this ordinary admission-first API:

{[
Observe.Logs.info (fun m -> m.text ~tag:"auth" "user logged in");

Observe.Logs.info (fun m ->
  let open Observe.Logs in
  m.untyped
  |+ m.field "action" Observe.Type.string "cart_validated"
  |+ m.field "items" Observe.Type.int item_count
  |> m.seal);

Observe.Logs.info (fun m ->
  m.typed ~using:checkout_schema
    { cart_id = "cart-42"; phase = Started })
]}

Use {!val:Observe.Logs.log} for a level computed at runtime. The fixed-level
PPX forms intentionally remain [debug], [info], [warn], and [error].

{1 Manual wide events}

An open wide event accepts sparse object contributions:

{[
let checkout = Observe.Logs.create ~name:"checkout" () in
[%observe.set checkout { cart_id = "cart-42"; phase = "started" }];
[%observe.set checkout { phase = "authorized"; authorization_id = "auth-7" }];
[%observe.info checkout "payment context completed"];
Observe.Logs.emit checkout
]}

Successive objects merge recursively. A later scalar or variant replaces the
earlier value at that field. [annotate] adds timestamped messages to the final
wide event; it is not a point log. The first [emit] seals and attempts
publication at most once.

A schema-locked wide event accepts generated sparse record patches:

{[
let checkout =
  Observe.Logs.create_typed ~name:"checkout" ~using:checkout_schema ()
in
[%observe.set checkout typed { cart_id = "cart-42"; phase = Started }];
[%observe.set checkout typed { phase = Authorized "auth-7" }];
Observe.Logs.emit checkout
]}

No record field is mandatory at completion. The schema constrains every field
that is contributed; it does not turn a wide event into one eagerly constructed
complete record.

{1 Scoped operations and children}

{[
let capture_payment () =
  let child = Observe.Logs.current () in
  [%observe.set child { provider = "example-pay"; status = "authorized" }];
  Lwt.return_unit

let checkout () =
  let parent = Observe.Logs.current () in
  [%observe.set parent { cart_id = "cart-42"; phase = "authorizing" }];
  Observe_lwt_unix.fork ~name:"capture-payment" capture_payment

let run () =
  Observe_lwt_unix.with_operation ~name:"checkout" checkout
]}

The child has its own occurrence identifier, fields, level, duration, and
lifecycle. It records the parent's name and occurrence identifier but copies no
parent payload. Parent, child, and correlated point logs remain independent
observations and retention units. The ready Lwt-Unix package generates UUID v4
identifiers by default; another runtime composition may choose a different
valid identity scheme.

Outside a valid scope, [current] raises
{!exception:Observe.Logs.Current_error}; it never returns a process fallback.

{1 Explicit errors}

An error interpreter keeps the consumer's own error type:

{[
let payment_error =
  Observe.Error.create (function
    | Payment_declined code ->
        Observe.Error.roles ~kind:"payment_declined" ~code ()
    | exn ->
        Observe.Error.roles ~kind:(Printexc.exn_slot_name exn)
          ~message:(Printexc.to_string exn) ())
]}

Use it as a point event:

{[
[%observe.error error ~using:payment_error ~backtrace exn]
]}

Or pass [~error:payment_error] to a scoped operation. Error contribution derives
[Error] unless an explicit wide level was selected. Observe interprets only an
error supplied at an explicit operation boundary; it installs no ambient
exception hook.

The complete generated syntax, manual schema construction, containers,
recursion, variants, and Repr interoperability are documented in the source
PPX reference.
